A||N||6||polomeno||engine swap overheating.. please help||||1||
Z||000000||polomeno||02-07-2001||12:39 PM||||Hey guys... this weekend I swapped my old 84 duke for a rebuild one I bought off Bubbajoexx, after the engine was in everything was going fine.. but if i drive over 80 km/hour  (aprox 40-45 miles ) i get a rather large coolant leak that somehow drips onto the exhaust and smokes up at the same time sucking in air which causes a air lock in the pump and the temp shoots up to 270 very fast,  we tried pressure tests and the leak does not show up.. we ran the engine idle for along time and no leak.. only when i drive.. we already changed the water pump and the hoses look good.. we're thinking either bad head gasket or cracked block.. any idea's anyone??<P>thanks<P>John<p>[This message has been edited by polomeno (edited 02-07-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000001||Phil||02-07-2001||02:04 PM||||A compression check will tell you about the head/block.||||reg||1
Z||000002||polomeno||02-07-2001||06:15 PM||||any idea's guys??||||reg||1
Z||000003||theogre||02-07-2001||06:52 PM||||make sure you don't have the "vented" radiator cap.<P>compression test. ||||reg||1
Z||000004||polomeno||02-07-2001||07:17 PM||||no i dont think it's a vented cap cause it's the same cap and thermoastat i used on my old engine without a problem.. some where fluid is just pissin  out when the car goes over 80km  or so it is hitting the exhaust and smokin up..<P>thanks<P>John<P><BR>[This message has been edited by polomeno (edited 02-07-2001).]<p>[This message has been edited by polomeno (edited 02-07-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||LuckyTheyWereEverMade||02-08-2001||12:02 AM||||Check your thermostat housing.  The flange that mounts to the head on an iron duke can warp.  This happened on my car and it used to leak all the time.<P>The waterneck is close to sitting above the catylitic converter, so if it leaks you will see some smoke.<P>While your in there, check the hose from the intake manifold to the passenger side rear of the engine compartment.  It runs down and then attaches to a steel pipe on the rear wall.  I found a bad leak there too.  <P>I had a thermostat cap that looked new  but leaked anyway.  I replaced it and the puddle of coolant on the transmission never reappeared.  These parts have more than half of their seals facting the front of the engine compartment, so seeing these leaks is not easy.  I could not confirm the leaks until I took the parts off the car.  I could see coolant on the decklid and transmission and little else.<P>I hope you seal your leak soon, dogs love coolant and it kills them.  We have a cool dog and I hate to hear about cars leaking coolant.<P> ||||reg||1
Z||000006||Formula88||02-11-2001||08:31 PM||||Make sure you get Sierra or another "earth friendly" coolant. They even have some that are called "low-toxic".  It's not non-toxic, but shouldn't kill most animals that would drink it.  It's also better on the environment.  It should work just fine in your car, and I believe it's 100% compatible, so you don't have to flush your system.  Note: this is different than the orange Dexcool 5 year coolant. That stuff has been known to disolve radiator hoses!  I know, it's done it to my 99 Grand Am.  Now if I can only figure out a way to put the 3400SFI engine in my GA into my Fiero....<P>------------------<BR>We all come into this world the same way; naked, screaming, and covered in blood.  If you're lucky, it doesn't have to end there.||||reg||3
